@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  child driver, child port, attach command, session export, starter tools,
}
@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import { delegation }

component Kernel {
  goal {
    Keep the core of subzero small and free of any one child driver or
    parent adapter: child drivers and parent adapters plug in through Kernel, and
    core services reach a child driver only through it and never reach a parent
    adapter.
  }

  state {
    The *driver registry* maps each *host* name to the one child driver registered
    for that host. A *supported host* is a host name that has an entry in the
    driver registry.

    A *parent adapter* is an extension that connects one kind of parent, such as a
    Claude Code session, to the core. Kernel keeps the names of the registered
    parent adapters only to refuse a second registration of a name.

    An *entry point* is the start-up code of one process, such as the command
    line, the MCP server or a hook. It is outside the core, it is the code of a
    process that imports a particular child driver or parent adapter in order to
    register it, and it imports the ones its process needs, which may be none, and
    registers them with Kernel before the process takes its first request.
  }

  constraints {
    Kernel must not name, import or depend on any particular child driver or
    parent adapter. An entry point registers each of them with Kernel, and Kernel
    loads none of them by name. A child driver or a parent adapter that no entry
    point of a process registers is absent from that process.

    The driver registry must hold at most one child driver for a host. A second
    registration for a host must be refused with an error that names the host, and
    it must not replace the first child driver.

    A parent adapter must reach the core only through the delegation of
    DelegationService, and its collect, cancel and listing, and through AgentConfig
    for what it shows or decides, such as
    the agent file, the routable tiers, the routing settings and the error texts,
    and through Kernel only to list the supported hosts that it gives AgentConfig,
    and it must never be given a child driver. A parent adapter of one parent may
    depend on another parent adapter of the same parent. A child driver must never
    be given a parent adapter.

    No core service may name a particular child driver or parent adapter, or import
    from one. A core service reaches a child driver only through the child port
    that Kernel provides for a host, and the attach command, the session export and
    the starter tools that it offers.
  }

  cases {
    With the pi child driver registered for the host pi, the driver registry holds
    pi as a supported host, and a request for the child port of the host pi returns
    the child port of that child driver.

    A request for the child port of a host with no registered child driver returns
    an error that names that host and lists every supported host, and it starts no
    child run.

    A second child driver registered for the host pi is refused with an error that
    names pi, and the first child driver stays registered.

    A parent adapter registered under a name that another parent adapter already
    holds is refused with an error that names that name.

    In a process whose entry point registers no child driver for the host pi, a
    request for the child port of the host pi returns the error for a host with no
    registered child driver, although another process may support pi.
  }

  interface {
    Register a child driver for a host in the driver registry, refusing a second
    child driver for the same host.

    Provide the child port of the child driver registered for a host, and its
    attach command, its session export and its starter tools when that child driver
    offers them, and return an error that names the host and lists every
    supported host when no
    child driver is registered for it.

    List every supported host, so that a caller can check a host or name the
    supported hosts in an error.

    Register a parent adapter by name, refusing a second parent adapter of the same
    name.

    Take the registrations of an entry point, which registers every child driver
    and parent adapter its process needs before the process takes its first
    request.
  }

  decisions {
    Child drivers and parent adapters will keep multiplying, such as Claude and
    Codex children and pi or Codex parents. Registering each of them with a small
    Kernel, instead of letting a core service name one, keeps the core unchanged
    when one is added and lets the child port and the delegation stay the only
    seams. The driver registry holds one child driver for each host so that a
    lookup by the host of an agent definition is never ambiguous.
  }
}
